# Scaling and Load Balancing Your Architecture

## Overview

In this lab, you use **Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)** and **Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling**
to load balance and automatically scale your infrastructure.

- **ELB** automatically distributes incoming application traffic across multiple
  EC2 instances and Availability Zones, providing fault tolerance
- **Auto Scaling** maintains application availability by automatically scaling
  EC2 capacity in or out based on conditions you define — ideal for applications
  with stable or variable demand patterns

### Starting Architecture
```
Public Subnet
└── Web Server 1 (EC2)
```

### Final Architecture
```
                    ┌─── Application Load Balancer ───┐
                    │                                  │
         AZ 1 - Private Subnet 1        AZ 2 - Private Subnet 2
         └── EC2 Instance (ASG)         └── EC2 Instance (ASG)
```

---

## Objectives

By completing this lab, you will be able to:

- Create an AMI from an EC2 instance
- Create a load balancer
- Create a launch template and an Auto Scaling group
- Configure an Auto Scaling group to scale new instances within private subnets
- Use Amazon CloudWatch alarms to monitor infrastructure performance

---

## Duration

**Approximately 45 minutes**

---

## Getting Started

### Access the AWS Management Console

1. Choose **Start Lab** at the upper-right corner
2. Wait for the **AWS** indicator to turn green (lab is ready)
3. Choose **AWS** to open the AWS Management Console in a new browser tab

> Do not change the lab Region unless specifically instructed to do so.

---

## Task 1: Create an AMI for Auto Scaling

> An AMI saves the contents of the boot disk so that new instances can be
> launched with identical content.

1. In the search bar, type **EC2** and select it
2. In the left navigation pane, under **Instances**, choose **Instances**
3. Select **Web Server 1** — confirm it is in a **Running** state
4. From the **Actions** dropdown, choose **Image and templates → Create image**
5. Configure the following:

| Field                | Value                        |
|----------------------|------------------------------|
| Image name           | `Web Server AMI`             |
| Image description    | `Lab AMI for Web Server`     |

6. Choose **Create image**

> Note the **AMI ID** from the confirmation screen — it will be used when
> configuring the Auto Scaling group.

---

## Task 2: Create a Load Balancer

> The load balancer distributes traffic across multiple EC2 instances and
> Availability Zones.

1. In the left navigation pane, under **Load Balancing**, choose **Load Balancers**
2. Choose **Create load balancer**
3. Under **Application Load Balancer**, choose **Create**

### Basic Configuration

| Field               | Value    |
|---------------------|----------|
| Load balancer name  | `LabELB` |

### Network Mapping

| Field              | Value                                      |
|--------------------|--------------------------------------------|
| VPC                | `Lab VPC`                                  |
| Availability Zone 1| `Public Subnet 1`                          |
| Availability Zone 2| `Public Subnet 2`                          |

### Security Groups

- Remove the **default** security group
- Select **Web Security Group** (permits HTTP access)

### Create the Target Group

1. In the **Listeners and routing** section, choose **Create target group**
2. Configure the following on the new tab:

| Field              | Value              |
|--------------------|--------------------|
| Target type        | `Instances`        |
| Target group name  | `lab-target-group` |

3. Choose **Next** → **Create target group**
4. Close the Target groups tab and return to the Load Balancers tab
5. Choose **Refresh** next to the **Forward to** dropdown
6. Select **lab-target-group** from the dropdown
7. Choose **Create load balancer**

> Copy and save the **DNS name** of the load balancer — you will need it later.

---

## Task 3: Create a Launch Template

> A launch template defines the configuration Auto Scaling uses when
> launching new EC2 instances.

1. In the search bar, type **EC2** and select it
2. In the left navigation pane, under **Instances**, choose **Launch Templates**
3. Choose **Create launch template** and configure the following:

### Template Details

| Field                      | Value                                |
|----------------------------|--------------------------------------|
| Launch template name       | `lab-app-launch-template`            |
| Template version description | `A web server for the load test app` |
| Auto Scaling guidance      | Enable (check the box)               |

### AMI

- Choose the **My AMIs** tab → confirm **Web Server AMI** is selected

### Instance Configuration

| Field          | Value       |
|----------------|-------------|
| Instance type  | `t3.micro`  |
| Key pair       | `Don't include in launch template` |
| Security group | `Web Security Group` |

4. Choose **Create launch template**

---

## Task 4: Create an Auto Scaling Group

1. Select **lab-app-launch-template**
2. From the **Actions** dropdown, choose **Create Auto Scaling group**

### Step 1 — Name

| Field                  | Value                   |
|------------------------|-------------------------|
| Auto Scaling group name| `Lab Auto Scaling Group` |

### Step 2 — Network

| Field                       | Value                                                    |
|-----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| VPC                         | `Lab VPC`                                                |
| Availability Zones & Subnets| `Private Subnet 1 (10.0.1.0/24)` and `Private Subnet 2 (10.0.3.0/24)` |

### Step 3 — Load Balancing & Health Checks

| Field                    | Value                            |
|--------------------------|----------------------------------|
| Load balancing           | `Attach to an existing load balancer` |
| Target group             | `lab-target-group \| HTTP`        |
| Health check type        | `ELB`                            |

### Step 4 — Group Size & Scaling Policy

| Field              | Value |
|--------------------|-------|
| Desired capacity   | `2`   |
| Minimum capacity   | `2`   |
| Maximum capacity   | `4`   |
| Scaling policy     | `Target tracking scaling policy` |
| Metric type        | `Average CPU utilization` |
| Target value       | `50`  |

> Auto Scaling will maintain ~50% average CPU utilization, adding or removing
> instances automatically as load fluctuates.

### Step 5 — Tags

| Key    | Value          |
|--------|----------------|
| `Name` | `Lab Instance` |

5. Choose **Create Auto Scaling group**

> Two new EC2 instances will launch in private subnets across both
> Availability Zones to meet the desired capacity of 2.

> If you see an error about `t3.micro` not being available, repeat this task
> using `t2.micro` instead.

---

## Task 5: Verify That Load Balancing is Working

1. In the left navigation pane, under **Instances**, choose **Instances**
   - Two **Lab Instance** instances should be running (launched by Auto Scaling)
2. In the left navigation pane, under **Load Balancing**, choose **Target Groups**
3. Select **lab-target-group**
4. In the **Registered targets** section, wait until both instances show
   **Health status: healthy**

> A healthy status confirms the load balancer will send traffic to those instances.

5. Open a new browser tab, paste the **load balancer DNS name**, and press **Enter**

> The **Load Test application** should appear — confirming the load balancer
> is routing traffic correctly to the Auto Scaling group instances.

---

## Task 6: Test Auto Scaling

> You will generate CPU load to trigger the Auto Scaling alarm and cause
> new instances to launch.

### Step 1 — View CloudWatch Alarms

1. In the search bar, type **CloudWatch** and select it
2. In the left navigation pane, under **Alarms**, choose **All alarms**

Two alarms were automatically created by the Auto Scaling group:

| Alarm         | Condition                        | Action                    |
|---------------|----------------------------------|---------------------------|
| `AlarmHigh`   | CPU Utilization > 50%            | Add instances             |
| `AlarmLow`    | CPU Utilization < 50%            | Remove instances          |

3. Confirm **AlarmHigh** currently shows state **OK**

### Step 2 — Generate Load

1. Return to the browser tab with the **Load Test application**
2. Choose **Load Test** next to the AWS logo

> This generates high CPU load across all instances in the Auto Scaling group.
> Do not close this tab.

### Step 3 — Monitor Alarms

1. Return to the **CloudWatch** tab
2. Refresh every **60 seconds**
3. Within 5 minutes:
   - **AlarmLow** status changes to **OK**
   - **AlarmHigh** status changes to **In alarm**

> Once CPU exceeds 50% for more than 3 minutes, Auto Scaling launches
> additional instances (up to the maximum of 4).

### Step 4 — Verify New Instances

1. In the search bar, type **EC2** and select it
2. Under **Instances**, choose **Instances**

> More than two **Lab Instance** instances should now be running —
> Auto Scaling added them in response to the high CPU alarm.

---

## Task 7: Terminate the Web Server 1 Instance

> Web Server 1 was used only to create the AMI and is no longer needed.

1. Select **Web Server 1** (ensure it is the only instance selected)
2. From the **Instance state** dropdown, choose **Terminate instance**
3. Choose **Terminate**

---

## Optional Challenge: Create an AMI Using AWS CLI

1. Connect to one of your **Lab Instance** EC2 instances using
   **EC2 Instance Connect**
2. At the top of the lab page, choose **AWS Details → Show** to retrieve
   your AWS CLI credentials
3. Configure AWS credentials:

```bash
aws configure
```

4. Create an AMI using the AWS CLI — replace the values as needed:

```bash
aws ec2 create-image \
  --instance-id <your-instance-id> \
  --name "CLI-Created-AMI" \
  --description "AMI created via AWS CLI" \
  --no-reboot
```

> You can find your instance ID from the EC2 Instances console.

---

## Summary of Resources Created

| Resource            | Name / Value              | Purpose                                          |
|---------------------|---------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
| AMI                 | `Web Server AMI`          | Base image for Auto Scaling group instances      |
| Load Balancer       | `LabELB`                  | Distributes traffic across AZs and instances     |
| Target Group        | `lab-target-group`        | Routes load balancer traffic to EC2 instances    |
| Launch Template     | `lab-app-launch-template` | Defines instance config for Auto Scaling         |
| Auto Scaling Group  | `Lab Auto Scaling Group`  | Maintains 2–4 instances based on CPU utilization |
| CloudWatch Alarms   | `AlarmHigh` / `AlarmLow`  | Trigger scaling actions at 50% CPU threshold     |

---

## Key Concepts

- **AMI (Amazon Machine Image)** — a snapshot of an instance used as a
  template to launch identical new instances
- **Application Load Balancer** — operates at Layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS),
  routing requests across healthy registered targets
- **Launch Template** — defines the instance configuration that Auto Scaling
  uses when launching new instances
- **Target Tracking Policy** — Auto Scaling automatically adjusts capacity
  to keep a metric (CPU utilization) near the target value
- **CloudWatch Alarms** — monitor metrics and trigger Auto Scaling actions
  when thresholds are breached for a defined period

